in the new book good cops he spoke about  it recently at Roosevelt University in  Chicago for an hour 20 minutes hi good  evening my name is Heather dolmage and I  am the director of the Mansfield  Institute for social justice here at  Roosevelt University and I just want to  say a couple of words about the  Mansfield Institute and then I'll be  introducing Terry Johnson who will  introduce our speaker tonight professor  David Harris this year the Mansfield  Institute has been very active we have  done done work in the area of sexuality  and social justice we've done stuff on  the reparations movement we had a one  book one University where we invited all  members of the Roosevelt community to  read Barbara Ramsey's book Ella Baker in  the black freedom movement and then we  hosted weekly book discussions and it  culminated a couple weeks ago with the  Mansfield lectureship Barbara ramsby  came to speak about the book and it was  a wonderful event we will be doing the  one book one University again next year  and we are open to nominations so please  if you have books that you think are of  interest in the area of social justice  let us know we are very happy to be  hosting a conversation with Professor  Harris again tonight he came in the fall  for a series that we worked with Hull  House and Terry Johnson on race and  criminalization so we are happy that he  is back with us again tonight I would  again before we before David speaks I  would like to introduce Terry Johnson  she is Terry Johnson is the vice  president of public policy reform and  advocacy at Hull House and she is going  to come up now to say some words about  professor Harris and a one-one side note  or shall I say sidebar one side note the  bookstore is here with professor  Harris's books and they will be here  until 6:15 so if you want to purchase  the books just make sure that you do so  and then he will be signing books  afterwards and we have refreshments  please  help yourself they're in the back of the  room thank you Thank You Heather  I add my good evening to all of you and  thank you for coming out  we have as Heather said worked with the  Mansfield Institute for a couple of  programs about race and criminalization  and the reason we do that is the Jane  Addams Hall House Association has for  the last 115 years been committed to the  notion that in order to change the  people lives that we serve we have to  change the conditions in which they find  themselves so we have for the last four  years been taking a more intensive  systemic analysis of how these systems  impact the lives of our clients and so  in order to do that one of the systems  we looked at was the criminal justice  system I have known David Harris for  almost eight years actually based on  some work that we've done before in  other cities around the very question of  racial profiling and so I am honored to  yet again bring David before you because  not only is he an expert in his field  but he's also truly committed to making  some changes in this system so let me  give you a little bit of information  about David before I ask him to join me  at the podium  he is the bought professor of law and  values at the University of Toledo  College of Law and indeed is the  nation's leading authority on racial  profiling his book Profiles and  injustice why racial profiling cannot  work was published in 2002 to wide  acclaim in the mainstream media he is  also the author of the first and the  most influential scholarly articles  published on the subject and helped  representative John Conyers introduced  the federal traffic stops statistics Act  of 1997 try saying that fast the Act  which is actually based on David's work  was the first legislative proposal at  any level to attack the problem of  racial profiling and is the model for  many laws throughout the nation and in  particularly more than 20 states for  data collection efforts and hundreds and  for those of you who don't know this  Illinois is actually working on some  legislation at the state level that's  going to help us track racial profiling  David works widely with the media on  racial profiling criminal justice and  policing issues and in his new book he  showcases police departments that have  proven that we actually can have  effective policing without racial  profiling and therefore have respectful  and proper treatment of all of our  citizenry but no further ado I present  to you David Harris thank you thank you  so much Terry and Heather it's a great  pleasure to be here my thanks to the  Mansfield Institute for sponsoring our  event this evening and to the Jane  Addams Hull House it's great honor to be  associated with them and to come in as  their guest and it's great to see all of  you here tonight I'm going to talk about  a common conception in our country you  know it's widely believed that if a  person if a citizen wants to be safe in  this country if you want to be safe from  criminals if you want safe streets you  want to be safe from terrorists you have  to be willing to give up your rights  under the Constitution you have to be  willing to give the police more room to  give them greater latitude because  that's the only way that we can really  ensure our safety well as Benjamin  Franklin said so long ago those who  would give up liberty for safety deserve  neither one but I want to take it a  little bit further in our modern world  and say this it is not necessary for us  to give up the best of who we are as  Americans in order to be safe either  from criminals or terrorists my work  over the last five years has taken me  all over the country large cities and  small towns big police departments and  sheriff's departments and all kinds of  law enforcement agencies with different  missions and I have seen that there is  another way that this trade-off the  require  of a trade-off of your civil liberties  for safety is a myth and it's one that I  want to expose by telling you the  stories of the police departments that  are doing something different because by  observing them and in the course of  writing this book writing about them I  came to see that it is not just possible  it is happening now we are having we are  in an era where we can have effective  policing when I say effective policing I  mean policing that is effective against  crime that cuts crime at the same time  that it respects citizens those two  things are not in opposition in fact  they go hand in hand  so I'd like to start tonight by telling  you a story right here from Chicago  where we are tonight this is the story  about a man named Anthony Scalise  Anthony Scalise is a sergeant in the  Chicago Police Department he's been with  the police department for almost four  decades and Anthony Scalise was named in  1991 to the Civil Rights unit of the  Chicago Police Department and his  particular charge was to take action  against hate crimes I think you know  many police departments many many police  officers even might regard this  assignment as a backwater maybe a  demotion something like that but Scalise  knew better and he immediately seized  upon the possibilities in his job it's  important to remember that in 1991 the  concept of hate crimes was rather new  that this was something that people had  not thought about much before in big  city police departments the idea that if  you commit a crime that is motivated by  racial or religious or ethnic bias that  that is something special something a  little different and that it requires  different kinds of enforcement and maybe  ultimately different kinds of punishment  so Scalise  really  took his assignment and grabbed it and  ran with it and he began by doing the  things that make this kind of policing  work preventive policing Scalise began  to meet with all of the minority groups  in Chicago of course he met with people  in the african-american community in the  Latino communities  Chicago of course a very diverse City  more than one Latino community they come  from many different places and he didn't  stop there he also met with many of the  religious groups even some religious  groups that were newer to Chicago and in  each instance he met with these groups  he went out of his way he talked to them  he came to their meetings in each case  his message was the same we're here to  help you we want to know if you've  experienced an incident of bias or  biased crime a hate crime you come to us  we'll help you be our partners will be  your partners and he worked at this very  steadily over the years and had a lot of  success building relationships building  partnerships all as a way of setting up  preventive measures now I want to take  you kind of fast forward to the July 4th  weekend of 1999 this particular weekend  some of you in Chicago may remember this  Scalise got a call in the early evening  he was told come right up immediately to  West Rogers Park or been a shooting and  when he got there he found that there  were six shooting victims six people  who've been shot from a moving car and  he knew right away why he'd been called  as soon as he got there because he saw  the victims there all of them were in  distinctive Orthodox Jewish dress this  is in a Jewish community it was Friday  night the victims all seem to be headed  towards one of the neighborhood  synagogues  it looked like a bias crime it looked  like a hate crime right away and as soon  as he got there he talked to one of his  officers and they told him they had a  problem the problem was the victims  wouldn't talk to the police  nobody in the neighborhood who might  have seen something would talk to the  police  and Scalise knew why because of his long  work with the Jewish community he  understood this he knew it was Friday  night the beginning of the Jewish  Sabbath he knew that observant Jews on  Friday night all the way through the  Sabbath do not do anything that might be  construed as work and because of that  they were not going to talk to municipal  officials even police officers that  might be seen as work now as soon as  this realization set in almost the same  moment another call came in over the  police radio system there had been  another shooting in the nearby suburb of  Skokie a black man outside had been shot  this man died and then just a short time  after that another report from the  suburb of Northbrook a group of Asian  people had been shot at and this told  Scalise he had his worst nightmare right  in front of him  a serial hate crime something you hope  never to see but there it was and he  knew he needed information and he needed  it right now he also knew that until the  Jewish Sabbath ended there would be no  work and therefore no information now he  didn't know what to do about this but he  knew who to call he called a man named  Moshe Wolfe Moshe Wolff is a rabbi lived  in that community was also an Orthodox  Jew he was a chaplain still is as far as  I know a chaplain for the Chicago Police  Department Scalise went to Wolfe he  filled him in  is it can you help me can you help me  can you do something and wolf knew  exactly what to do he went right to the  scene right to where he could find the  victims right to where he could find the  witnesses and he talked to them wolf is  a rabbi he knew exactly what to say  he took the people aside and he  explained to them a Jewish concept  called Baku of nefesh but Kulik nefesh  translates roughly as a matter of life  and death and rabbi wolf explained to  these folks yes we keep the Sabbath we  observe it strictly but a matter of life  and death takes precedence and this is  one of those times and with rabbi Wolf's  encouragement the victims came forward  they went to the 24th district they gave  information about the shooter the car it  was the first fragmentary but the first  description of the shooter and the  vehicle and for the first time the  police were able to match that  information to information that came  from the other shootings they realized  they had the same person and it turned  into a crucial break in the case that  allowed the police to track this shooter  all the way down to downstate Illinois  where they later cornered him it turned  out he was a follower of Matthew Hale of  the World Church of the Creator the  white supremacist Church his name was  Benjamin Smith and it was because  Scalise had a partner it was because he  knew who to go to  that this case took the fortunate turn  that it did that is preventive policing  that is the way police departments can  go in the future to get ahead of crime  to be effective against crime and at the  same time to work with the public to  have them  as partners this idea that police need  more room and citizens need less  constitutional protection in order for  us to be safe Scalise is the proof that  that's not so and in this is not a  theory this is in practice it's on the  ground it's working its effective and  it's measurable preventive policing can  be the future so let me take a few  minutes and tell you just what  preventive policing is there are five  core principles to preventive policing  and if we understand that I think we'll  get a good grip on why it is such an  innovation why it's such an opportunity  first first core principle is building  bridges  this is Scalise building bridges  building partnerships and relationships  with the community so that when the time  comes they're there to be taken  advantage of and used this facilitates  communication and there is simply  nothing more important than  communication because the communication  it takes place between the community  served and the police who serve them  bring lots of important crime-fighting  pieces of information to the police and  they give the police the opportunity to  learn what the community's priorities  are number two problem solving when I  say problem solving what I mean is a  strategy of policing that doesn't focus  on individual incidents it's very easy  in today's world to to talk about how  overwhelmed police are with constant 911  with constant needs to respond and they  are overwhelmed they have more to do  than they can handle  problem solving policing takes a  different approach instead of seeing  each crime as an individual discrete  incident  problem-solving policing looks at the  whole picture pulls back and says well  why do we get so many calls from this  particular corner why so many 911 calls  from this apartment building let's  analyze the data let's look at the crime  patterns over the last 24 months who  were the people involved who were the  victims who were the perpetrators when  we caught them what seemed to be the  patterns here by focusing on patterns  police can devise solutions that will  actually cut down on the number of 911  calls over the long run it will actually  reduce crime and sometimes even  eliminate it from the hot spots in every  city in town  third accountability accountability both  external and internal I cannot emphasize  this strongly enough this is really the  linchpin of so many efforts to reform  and improve policing in so many cities  around the country  when I say external accountability what  I mean is accountability to citizens  when citizens file a complaint is there  an institution a mechanism a device in  place to handle complaints in an  independent way that citizens will trust  when I say internal accountability what  I'm talking about  are new kinds of mechanisms that have  come in to policing over the last 10  years the one that is the most important  in my mind is something we call an early  warning system an early warning system  is a way to use computer technology to  bring discrete pieces of information  about police behavior all together have  them all in one place in one information  system so that way the chief the captain  the lieutenant all the way down to the  sergeant can know what police are doing  individually and contract that behavior  now you'd expect things like citizens  complaints to be measured this way and  tracked this way you might expect thing  such as automobile accidents uses of  force shootings whatever it turns out to  be but there are lots of other things  that go into these systems to Mister  court dates sick days outside employment  all of this creates a total picture of  where any individual officer is at and  when the lieutenant the sergeant the  captain sees signs of trouble they can  nip that trouble in the bud they can get  the officer retrained they can get the  officer counsel they can send the  officer to substance abuse treatment  whatever it takes they can get things  back on track and what if what it takes  is nudging somebody into another line of  work they might be able to do that too  leadership changes for many many years  police leadership has been same-old  same-old in a military-style  organization you know police have ranks  there are lines of authority there are  reporting systems don't go over the  captain's head to the next guy and  increasingly there are new leaders in  police leaders who adapt and who have to  have brought to policing a different  style and a key ingredient here is  openness an openness to new ideas and  openness to outsiders and most most  different of all a willingness to share  power in police organizations for many  many years  the idea was accumulate power for  yourself no matter what your position  was you don't want to give it away and  an increasing number of Chiefs are  seeing that the thing to do is to give  their subordinates power along with  accountability and responsibility this  is a sea change in law enforcement  number five changes in police culture  this may be the toughest nut to crack of  all you know police culture is  notoriously insular police organizations  are famous for excluding the ideas and  views of outsiders there's a real  feeling that  you haven't worn the uniform or carried  the badge you don't know what the job is  like and you really have no right to  criticize I understand where that comes  from it is a singular kind of job but  ultimately it's a job in a democratic  society in which the public has to be  served this realization that is dawning  on an increasing number of police  departments is what is driving changes  in police culture and what I see  could be neatly encapsulated you know in  a nice little analogy I came across I  saw an article by by somebody the title  of it it was about changing police  culture in it then the article said  something like it's changing police  culture will be just as easy as bending  granite easy bending granite and I get  it  you know there's there's something to  that but what I also see is that now we  could not we could regard police culture  not as granite but maybe is wood wood is  hard wood is firm but wood can be bent  if you take the time you apply the  pressure correctly a strong straight  piece of wood can become the beautiful  curving wood of the guitar or a violin  and that's the kind of thing it will  take to change police culture strong  consistent pressure over a long time a  demand that things be done in a new and  better way and now that there are models  out there this is no longer a pipe dream  so those are the five core ideas  building bridges solving problems  accountability leadership changes and  changes in police culture I think what  I'd like to do is to just illustrate one  of these for you using something I saw  while I was researching and writing the  book because when you go out and you  look at these police departments  and you see what they're actually doing  it really is remarkable it really shows  you that change is possible in an area  where we often thought it was hopeless  so let me take a few minutes and tell  you about Stamford Connecticut Stamford  Connecticut this is a medium-sized city  outside of New York and in the middle to  late 90s they got a new chief Chiefs  name is Dean Esserman  chief Esserman  was known as an original thinker in  policing a person who was open to ideas  so one of the reasons I like this story  is it brings a number of different  principles together one of the things  that chief Esserman did when he started  in Stanford was he began a school police  officer program lots of communities have  these programs they are formally known  as SRO program school resource officer  programs there is funding available  there's training available there are  standards things like that set out by  federal agencies and organizations  Esserman liked this idea he liked the  fact that police would be able to  connect with the children in the  community that they'd be able to connect  with the community through the schools  and he decided he wanted to have a  program of school resource officers in  his town so he picked out his officers  and the people he wanted for this  program he did not go for what you might  think as you know the conventional  choices he didn't look for people who  had maybe special skills with kids he  went for his toughest biggest most  experienced cops cops and two of the  many came up with were Scott Baldwin and  John Jeter they're fascinating guys  Baldwin had been on the dive team of the  department he'd been in a SWAT unit I  mean this was an action-oriented police  officer  no mistaking it John Jeter had been in  Newark in the narcotics squad for 15  years very successful and so nobody  could say that these were cops who  couldn't do the whole job that's why a  sermon wanted them that's why he  selected them well these officers along  with eight others went into the Stanford  schools and the program was an immediate  success the community loved it schools  loved it and the kids loved it but maybe  most surprising the cops loved it the  police officers just loved it and you  talked to John Jeter about this and he  he says you know this this was the  greatest thing that ever happened to me  I was in narcotics for 15 years I was  you know out there on the street I was  making a bus here I got a chance to  prevent future crime I got a chance to  get ahead and that's just not something  you can do when you're around the  narcotics squad John Jeter was posted to  a middle school and he became one of the  most popular police officers in Stanford  well he went through his first year well  into the second year and chief Esserman  held a meeting with the school resource  officers toward the end of that second  year he held periodic meetings with all  his officers so he'd get to see them  talk to them take take stock of their  experience get their ideas and he was  known as being an original thinker in a  kind of open-minded chief so when chief  Esserman  said to the school resource officers at  this meeting what are you going to do  this summer  John Jeter decided to take a chance  because he had a wild idea he decided to  let this idea out and see how the chief  reacted to it he said chief how about we  start a summer camp how about we have a  summer camp for the kids in my school  and he explained you know he said you  know these are middle school kids the  ones who go to summer school will go to  summer school till noon every day by the  time it's noon  it's too late for them to go to the  regular camps they all start in the  morning they're too young to get summer  jobs the too old for the daycare stuff  so what happens to them loose on the  street loose at home parents still at  work it's a recipe for disaster  I want to figure out something we can do  for my kids my kids he says hundreds of  my kids and I want to have a camp we  could have it here at school we can give  the kids lunch you know we'll do  something with them we'll figure it out  now I think that in most police  departments the idea of let's run a  summer camp for middle schoolers might  have seemed crazy I think a lot of  Chiefs would have reacted badly to this  what are you nuts we're cops we're not  camp counselors what do you think this  is but estimate is a different kind of  chief and his reaction instantly was  let's do it this is a great idea and he  said about building on the relationships  that he had already cultivated in his  two or so years as chief he went to the  mayor and got a commitment of city funds  so that they could hire high school kids  and college kids as counselors pay him a  good wage give him something to put on a  resume give him training he went to a  local foundation a Domus foundation this  foundation had been sponsoring youth  programs in Stanford for a long time  they were the right organization because  they could supply the administrative  structure for the camp as you know  police don't really know how to run a  camp but these people do they went to  the to the City School District they got  the district to commit the use of one of  their buildings they got the bus service  committed and they got private sector  money to fill out the picture and last  but not least a sermon ever the taker of  chances he takes his drug forfeiture  money which is supposed to go to buy  fancy equipment for his department says  just going into camp and I don't care  what you say about it  and so the camp was often running in his  first year it serviced a hundred kids  the next year more the year after that  even more by the third year was taking  every kid in town that wanted to be in  this camp I visited this camp it was  really something to see you know the  kids are well occupied they're doing all  the things that you do in a camp some  are taking martial arts and some are  doing computers and some are doing arts  and crafts and some are outside playing  ball but they all are doing something  and they all are doing it with people  who can be good strong adult role models  and it was really something to see all  these high school kids the college kids  they all had good jobs for the summer  there was competition to get these jobs  after the first year it was a real  success so you ask yourself how is this  crime fighting how does this fight crime  it's a legitimate question after the  first two years the United States  Department of Justice Unit came in and  did an evaluation of the camp they found  that kids were more able to resist gang  recruitment drug use had better  relationships with the police and that  the police had better relationships not  only with the kids but with the parents  and the community that the relationships  that had flowered during the school year  actually got even better but again you  ask yourself show me that it works for  cutting crime is this crime fighting or  is it social work is it camp counseling  well let me show you we have a very  interesting opportunity to learn  something from this experience because  after the first several years that the  camp was in operation in 2002 the bottom  fell out of the economy  and Stamford Connecticut as it did in  many other cities in the country private  sector money for the camp completely  dried up the city administration had no  extra money all the school district  could do would be to commit what was to  commit the school building and even the  foundation the Domus foundation was  unable to do anything but a kind of  scaled-down effort and so when the camp  was looking at the summer of 2003 things  looked pretty bleak and what they  decided that they wanted to do was to  preserve the structure of the camp but  it would have to be in a much  scaled-down sighs so the camp of 2003  was less than 20 percent the size of the  camp of 2002 they were down to about 35  kids and these were the 35 kids were  most at risk that we're going to the  at-risk summer program and what this  does for us standing back now is it  gives us an opportunity to ask a pretty  interesting question no it's not a  controlled experiment but if we can get  the numbers we can actually see what  difference the camp might have made so  let me show you some of these numbers  in the summer of 2001 in Stanford there  were 69 arrests of juveniles kids in  this age bracket for two hundred and  thirty six crimes in the summer of 2002  the trajectory which had been started  actually before what you see here was  clearly downward for only fifty seven  arrests for a hundred and seventy five  total crimes now we look at 2003 the  year in which the camp was vastly scaled  back a hundred and five juvenile arrests  for four hundred and fourteen separate  crimes now I'm not going to stand up  here and make the logical mistake of  saying just because something happened  after it happened because of but I have  looked and looked I have to tell you  four other explanations of why crime  suddenly spiked up in this age group in  this particular summer and I can't find  one it seems at least a question worth  asking at least something worth  exploring and I'm hoping that as we go  forward to 2005 the camp is going to be  up and running again at full strength  because this was a perfect example of  problem solving by a police officer  Jeter looks out he sees a problem he  sees hundreds of kids with nothing to do  for five hours a day before at least  some of their parents come home between  12:00 and 5 he knows that's the most  crime prone hours for this age group  this is when kids get into trouble he  looks at the whole picture and he says  what would change this and what would  allow me to solidify my relationships  with my kids and he comes up with this  idea the camp  this is problem-solving and preventive  policing at its absolute finest and it's  rightly regarded as a national example  of the kind of things that police can do  when they're allowed creativity when  they're told big picture  take a look ahead prevent don't just  react so much of our policing for so  long has been about reacting to crime  reacting after its occurred now that  will never go away they're always going  to be emergencies it's always going to  be 911 police respond to that's never  going to go away but what we can look  forward to with preventive policing is a  future where that's not all the police  do and they put an increasing amount of  effort to reducing crime over the long  haul in ways that will frankly make for  fewer emergencies as we go on now let's  talk about today's world and the  question that's on everybody's mind what  about terrorism does preventive policing  have a role in preventing terrorism how  is it stack up there and the short  answer is preventive policing prevention  is what the whole effort is about with  terrorism that's what the whole game is  you know I hope that they will catch  Osama bin Laden at some point I hope  that will happen I hope he faces justice  for what he did I hope all the people  that helped him do that will face  justice but frankly the damage is done  whether they catch him or not  what would be really good is to be able  to head off further terrorist attacks  prevent them whether we catch people  afterwards when the damage has been done  much better to catch him beforehand so  prevention is the right focus and it  turns out that preventive policing is  exactly the strategy  that can do this for us and that  reactive types of policing don't help at  all and in fact may even hurt us now  when we say preventive policing and  terrorism the most important thing the  most a number one issue for preventive  policing for prevention of terrorism is  information I don't mean anything fancy  here you can call it intelligence if  you're the CIA and the National Security  Agency  I mean information in the sense that  police officers use that word on the  ground you want to know what's going on  in your community you have to talk to  the people in your community you have to  have relationships with them you have to  have a two-way information conduit  between the people you serve and the  people doing the serving information is  the key the absolute essential to  heading off terrorism think about it I  mean if we have sleeper cells on our  soil right now if there are cells in  this country made up of middle-eastern  adherents of Osama bin Laden if they're  there and a government says they are  what we need is information there are  only a couple of ways to figure out what  these people are up to one is  infiltrating them but since the FBI has  fired all its arabic-speaking agents and  has trouble with its translators and  just can't recruit people from these  communities that's out so we have one  choice there is only one possibility for  getting this information just one we  have to get it from the communities  themselves they are the people who know  the language they are the people who  know the culture they are the people who  understand the nuances of what people  say and how they express themselves  who's moving in who's moving out who's  keeping a low profile  some odd way who's got suspicious  opinions whatever it happens to be the  people in the community are the only  ones that we can turn to now we need the  middle eastern communities like we have  never needed anyone in fact for  prevention of terrorism how are we going  to accomplish this preventive policing  build those bridges solve problems with  the community be accountable to them you  make a mistake say so when you have  statistics that show the performance of  your department put them out there let  the public react to them take complaints  seriously and hold your officers  accountable for them those are the  things that will allow people to begin  to trust you  and to treat you as a partner if you're  the police department and that's what  will bring you the information and  frankly it's the only thing that can  bring you the information because if the  people in the community don't trust the  police they'll fear the police and that  will break down that conduit of  information let me give you an example  of how this works we're all familiar I  hope with the cell that