f domestic violence people  won't come to them they won't call the  police they won't even call the fire  department if they think they're going  to get reported so there's been a real  strong effort over the last five years  by local police in many places to show  the immigrant communities we're on your  side we don't care about that stuff we  want to make our city safe because we  are all against the Predators who are  out there and what the federal effort to  push local police into immigration work  will do is destroy these efforts set  them back if good policing preventive  policing is about building bridges this  will be about burning bridges and once  that's done you can't get it back other  examples the federal government's effort  to question 5,000 young men from the  Arab world where in the United States  began in fall of 2001 another 3,000 in  the fall of 2002 why do they want to  question them well they're not suspects  it's all voluntary they might just might  know something  and while the Attorney General can give  orders to the FBI to go out there and do  this  they asked because they could not force  they asked local police to get involved  too because only the local police would  know their communities well enough to be  able to go into those communities and  find the people the Department of  Justice was seeking  and the local police departments did not  sign up some did but there was a  surprising amount of opposition some of  it out loud some of it very quiet lots  of big city and small town police  chief's saying you know what we don't  want any part of this you got to do this  you go out and do it yourself  we don't want it because this will just  put us on the wrong side and all that  information that we've been getting will  begin to disappear and we just don't  want that and we're going to be the ones  we the local police will be the ones who  in five years or ten years end up on the  wrong side of the lawsuit that gets  brought because of th