ered in New  York so that's why we all know that  story there are legitimate reasons to  wonder whether that's what was actually  responsible for bringing crime down in  New York that's a different talk for a  different day but what I can tell you  for sure is that there's a new way of  doing things that we don't have to put  up with massive numbers of arrests and  tough tough tough enforcement in order  to have safer streets and I saw the  proof of it over and over and over in  many places in this country so the first  step is to understand it the second is  to support it when you see things like  this happening in your community when  you see police officers come into  community meetings and commit it over  the long term and willing to sit down  and listen to the community when you see  that you need to support it you need to  talk to your City Council people and say  you know the following happened last  week that's good I want to see more of  that  you know the officer got out of his car  and he talked with those five kids on  the corner instead of giving them an  order out of the PA he had a  conversation with them I want to see  more of that that took that took guts to  do that and I want to see more of it  when you do that you have a chance of  getting it and last but not least there  is simply no substitute for demanding it  if we don't demand better police work we  won't get it now  oftentimes that will take the form of  demanding that certain practices stop  that excessive use of force be stopped  that there be new training so that the  worst that policing sometimes brings to  us will go away will be eliminated but  it takes more than that you have to be  able to say not only do I not want this  I want this  and the examples of preventive policing  that I've been talking about are the  kinds of things that everybody in this  country can support and must demand  because that's the only way we're going  to get it now the good thing is I found  lots of police