ody has  an interest in that this goes back to  something else I said  there's a feeling that if you haven't  been on the job you haven't worn the  uniform you don't have standing to  criticize therefore civilian review  really has no place I simply don't agree  with that in fact civilian review is  necessary for the credibility of  policing for the public to believe in  policing to have faith in its police  department to have face that faith that  its officers are in the same team as the  public is that kind of credibility is  crucial and without accountability you  just don't have it  next question this man there's been like  a long history of bad blood so to speak  in negativity between the police and the  residents what are some of the things  they police can do to signal that they  are in fact trying to go about a  different way because there's such a  deep mistrust of the officers and let me  repeat the question that's a great  question in many communities a strong  historical distrust exists between  police and communities and it runs both  ways it really does in a situation like  that what can police departments do to  recover some of that trust - to start  over again if you like to build upon it  in order to make some of these  preventive measures possible it's a very  good question and it's a very difficult  thing to do but it's not impossible I  would point to the example of Boston a  lot has been written about the very  successful efforts in Boston to curb  juvenile homicides with guns but this  was part of a larger picture if you go  back to Boston in the late 80s and early  90s there was really almost no city in  the country in which there was deeper  distrust between police and the  african-american community than in  Boston I bet a lot of you remember the  story of Charles Stewart and his wife  I'll just remind you this was a white  man who's at the hospital with his  pregnant wife also white  he shot his wife in their car shot  himself reported to the police a black  guy jumped out was trying to rob us and  shot us both the police took you know  took his report as gospel and began to  go through Roxbury and all the the other  black neighborhoods in Boston just  turned every young black man upside down  you know just really made a scene of it  and in the end of course what had  happened was the Charles Stewart had  done this and he threw himself over over  a bridge into the Charles River so he  never had to face the consequences but  it was this and a number of other very  very poor incidents that really had  police and community relations in Boston  at a very low point in the early 90s and  then at the same time they began to face  this terrible problem with their kids  killing each other shooting each other  with handguns and there was a hundred  and fifty such killings in one of the  worst years alone and they had no idea  what to do the old methods that you know  going to the neighborhoods with you know  like a bulldozer and frisk everybody and  sweep the corners none that stuff is  working so maybe what it takes is  getting to a point where you're just  searching for answers because what they  came up with was a unique combination of  resources and people they brought  together not just the highest levels of  law enforcement in the city of Police  Department the United States attorney  the district attorney the probation  department which never usually has  anything to do with the police  department believe it or not along with  the faith community the 10-point  coalition a coalition of black ministers  people in a school system lots and lots  of people and they got together and they  said what do we really have here what do  we really have here it was a  problem-solving workshop essentially  that covered many months what they did  was they figured out what the problem  was  took a one step at a time it turned out  you look at the statistics you looked at  the shootings you looked at the  incidents what you had was a hardcore  small number of gang members it wasn't  even all the gang members it was just  10% they were causing all this havoc so  they brought these people around the  table all the people from the community  from law enforcement they put together a  rounded effort they didn't just go out  and round people up what they did was  they created alternatives anybody wants  to get out of the gang life we can find  you a GED program and get you back into  high school and find you a job we can  get you trained we can do something for  you if you want to get out this is your  opportunity with the hardcore 10% those  people were called in a few at a time  they sat in a room with this  high-powered law enforcement team and  they were told in no uncertain terms no  sheriff in town things are going to  change there's going to be no more guns  we catch you with a gun got you're  shooting a gun catch you're shooting at  somebody with a gun you are going to do  federal time will not be down with  people from the neighborhood and the  county jail you will not go into Western  mass into one of those jails you'll be  out some godforsaken place in Montana or  Nevada where you don't know nobody  everybody's bigger than you and they  hate your guts and this got people's  attention within a month these homicides  went directly downward for two and a  half years there were no homicides by  gun between juveniles now they're more  than zero now things have not stayed  that peaceful they're back to a level  that is not tolerable  but is a little more normal than where  they were the point is this folks in the  community and the police thought they  had nothing in common they thought  they'd had it with each other and by  working together to solve the problem by  asking themselves what's the real  problem here they  they had common ground and they were  able to break through all of that Boston  is now one of the places that other  police departments come to to ask how do  we handle a problem like this so it can  be done it takes work it takes  commitment takes patience it takes time  you know you don't get into situations  of deep distress like that overnight you  don't get out of them overnight either  as people who work in that community  where that historic ingrained mistrust  has been there and it takes us a very  long time to even gain the trust to come  into the community to do the work that  we need to do or that we want to do from  the whole house how do we bring these  two people together because we actually  straddle working with the police even  getting the police to acknowledge us  that we're coming into these  developments this community to do good  stuff and the residents are trusting us  to do this stuff without kind of them  thinking we're telling on you we're  telling you what they're doing so where  does it start  because we we seem to get to a point and  then we just stand there you know we  just get a police officer to come in to  show good faith see this is officer  so-and-so and she's working with us from  CPD but that's as far as it goes  that police officer can't then take it  back to a bigger arena even in a station  you know which where she works from to  help us continue to do the work that  needs to be done because primary is what  you're talking about to get the police  to do preventive stuff and to gain the  support and trust of that community it's  almost impossible yes a very good  question I don't want to do too much to  reframe it because I think you really  hit so many things on the head there  first of all let me say in reaction I  honor your service and the work you do  it's extremely difficult you're taking  risks you're going out on a limb  personally and I recognize that and I  honor what you're doing for your  communities and without people like you  know  thing would happen so the first step has  actually already been taken and it's  been taken by you and I think everybody  owes you thanks how do we get past that  it's tough but the only way that that  can happen is if there's real commitment  on the part of the police department  you've shown coming from Hull House  coming from the community  you've shown you're willing to step up  offer your hand get involved it can't  end with the department simply sending  an officer for a meeting that is not  enough if the police department and the  other citizens in this city want to see  real change they want to see crime rates  go down and stay down and they want to  see that happen not just in their own  neighborhoods but across the city in  neighborhoods where they don't live  there has to be a citywide commitment  the police department must must follow  through on those things if they don't it  will fail that's the surest thing I can  tell you so I don't have a real  optimistic message I just say takes work  and the work has to come from both sides  you're doing it let's see some more  question over here  my question has to do with your attitude  that's the wrong word with your view of  the Justice Department's efforts to  involve local police departments in  their work and I was in I was just  wondering if there is any change or if  there's any movement in the in the  Justice Department or the federal  government  to see your point of view and to okay to  adjust or modify those rather stringent  directives that you that you that you  criticized so much sure the question is  is there any change of foot any prospect  of things getting different in terms of  how the Justice Department is seeking to  use and work with local police I do see  a little bit of change but I see it on  the ground  I see it in the FBI field offices I  talked about Dearborn before so it seems  only fair to start there the FBI office  in the Eastern District of Michigan  where Dearborn is near Detroit has been  very active with trying to work with the  Muslim community to do things with them  and in fact I know that the the special  agent in charge of the FBI field office  until recently and the fellow who was  the United States Attorney these are  people who are in the supervisory roles  on the ground they nominated for  instance one of the Arab Americans most  communities most prominent activists for  a special Justice Department award to  recognize the great work he had done  mobilizing his community to work with  the FBI so they wanted to recognize him  to further pull these efforts together  and I'm sorry to report that this did  not work out the way that either of  them hoped the FBI director director  Muller rejected this nomination after it  had been announced for undisclosed  reasons reasons that still aren't  disclosed but of course leave the kind  of patina that this man may have been  somehow vaguely connected with terrorism  is a terrible thing and as you can guess  it really worsened relations in a way  that you almost can't imagine so I see  the people on the ground really trying  to do things better trying to go into  those communities and and create the  relationships and so forth they're not  getting a great deal of support in  Washington from what I can tell now the  obvious question that kind of pops into  your mind is what about now John  Ashcroft is gone Albert Alberto Gonzalez  is the Attorney General maybe things  will improve I see him as a sort of  kinder gentler Attorney General he says  I read that he's reaching out to the  Justice Department senior staff the  career people that scene is a good thing  but you know in his first major policy  address on these questions in the end of  February  he basically announced that he was  supporting every initiative that the  prior Attorney General had put forward  so the best I can tell you on the top  level is wait and see I don't see any  evidence that things have changed yet  your to your to the question from the  lady from hull-house I gathered that  municipal and police leadership is  essential to preventive policing and  then when I hear your story about which  I read about in the book and Stamford  Connecticut  I'm concerned because we are in an era  where municipalities and police  departments frankly are struggling to  come up with enough money just to pay  their police officers and there's a  great aspect of print preventative  policing that frankly requires money and  in in this era how what's your honest  forecast in terms of how do we sell this  to municipal leaders into police leaders  who are struggling to come up with  enough pennies to pay their officers  much less put those officers in  alternative programs or paid for  alternative programs and whatnot it's an  excellent question where is the money  going to come from you know in American  society that's always something we have  to think about and it's a very live  question right now because the federal  budget which is in front of Congress  which came from President Bush some  weeks back that budget supplies a good  deal of seed money for programs like the  ones I've been talking about and for  reasons that really escaped me these  programs and others are on the chopping  block they have been either zero funded  or ninety percent cut things like that  so it's clear that the rescue is not  going to come from the federal  government in fact the opposite is going  to happen how do you sell it you have to  sell it and I don't think it's any it's  any stretch to sell it as public safety  it is public safety  these aren't extras these aren't add-ons  these are ways to create and maintain  public safety over the long run great  example I heard last week I was  listening to the news there is a group  in California that is proposing  mandatory I'm not sorry not mandatory  but universal preschool preschool for  every four-year-old kid it would be  universally available it's now of course  available to those  kanpei but this would make it available  to everybody the strongest supporters of  this of this initiative and it's going  to be on the