ase that you  cited initially with Matt Hale and his  followers and what are the you know  where immigrant communities are actually  the target of that terrorism and so what  kind of preventive policing is happening  around the the Tim mcveigh's and the  Matt Hales and that sort of the same  principles apply that's a terrific  question because as you know before  September 11th the greatest terrorist  act terrorism on American soil was  perpetrated by Tim McVeigh  the all-american Army veteran and unless  we pay attention to those communities we  won't get the information we need there  either it's all about information it's  all about connections it's all about  looking at the full picture so that when  somebody is deeply dissatisfied with  their life their treatment by the  government something like this somebody  comes forward now is that going to  happen every time no we can't prevent  all crimes but if we focus these same  kinds of efforts not just on getting  information from this community of that  one but across the board if we make this  the style of policing the manner of  policing the strategies for policing we  use everywhere will be way ahead and  we'll have a better future whether  violence comes out of this group or that  group won't be as important as what  we've done already to put things in  place to help us when the time comes I  thank you all it's been a real pleasure  I thank the Mansfield Institute and Hull  House for hosting the event thank you  very much thank you  thank you David Harris is a law  professor at the university of toledo  college of law good cops the case for  preventive policing is published by the  new press on the web at the new press

