ack of the  room thank you Thank You Heather  I add my good evening to all of you and  thank you for coming out  we have as Heather said worked with the  Mansfield Institute for a couple of  programs about race and criminalization  and the reason we do that is the Jane  Addams Hall House Association has for  the last 115 years been committed to the  notion that in order to change the  people lives that we serve we have to  change the conditions in which they find  themselves so we have for the last four  years been taking a more intensive  systemic analysis of how these systems  impact the lives of our clients and so  in order to do that one of the systems  we looked at was the criminal justice  system I have known David Harris for  almost eight years actually based on  some work that we've done before in  other cities around the very question of  racial profiling and so I am honored to  yet again bring David before you because  not only is he an expert in his field  but he's also truly committed to making  some changes in this system so let me  give you a little bit of information  about David before I ask him to join me  at the podium  he is the bought professor of law and  values at the University of Toledo  College of Law and indeed is the  nation's leading authority on racial  profiling his book Profiles and  injustice why racial profiling cannot  work was published in 2002 to wide  acclaim in the mainstream media he is  also the author of the first and the  most influential scholarly articles  published on the subject and helped  representative John Conyers introduced  the federal traffic stops statistics Act  of 1997 try saying that fast the Act  which is actually based on David's work  was the first legislative proposal at  any level to attack the problem of  racial profiling and is the model for  many laws throughout the nation and in  particularly more than 20 states for  data collection efforts and hundreds and  for those of you who don't know this  Illinois is actually working on some  legislation at the state level that's  going to help us track racial profiling  David works widely with the media on  racial profiling criminal justice and  policing issues and in his new book he  showcases police departments that have  proven that we actually can have  effective policing without racial  profiling and therefore have respectful  and proper treatment of all of our  citizenry but no further ado I present  to you David Harris thank you thank you  so much Terry and Heather it's a great  pleasure to be here my thanks to the  Mansfield Institute for sponsoring our  event this evening and to the Jane  Addams Hull House it's great honor to be  associated with them and to come in as  their guest and it's great to see all of  you here tonight I'm going to talk about  a common conception in our country you  know it's widely believed that if a  person if a citizen wants to be safe in  this country if you want to be safe from  criminals if you want safe streets you  want to be safe from terrorists you have  to be willing to give up your rights  under the Constitution you have to be  willing to give the police more room to  give them greater latitude because  that's the only way that we can really  ensure our safety well as Benjamin  Franklin said so long ago those who  would give up liberty for safety deserve  neither one but I want to take it a  little bit further in our modern world  and say this it is not necessary for us  to give up the best of who we are as  Americans in order to be safe either  from criminals or terrorists my work  over the last five years has taken me  all over the country large cities and  small towns big police departments and  sheriff's departments and all kinds of  law enforcement agencies with different  missions and I have seen that there is  another way that this trade-off the  require  of a trade-off of your civil liberties  for safety is a myth and it's one that I  want to expose by telling you the  stories of the police departments that  are doing something different because by  observing them and in the course of  writing this book writing about them I  came to see that it is not just possible  it is happening now we are having we are  in an era where we can have effective  policing when I say effective policing I  mean policing that is effective against  crime that cuts crime at the same time  that it respects citizens those two  things are not in opposition in fact  they go hand in hand  so I'd like to start tonight by telling  you a story right here from Chicago  where we are tonight this is the story  about a man named Anthony Scalise  Anthony Scalise is a sergeant in the  Chicago Police Department he's been with  the police department for almost four  decades and Anthony Scalise was named in  1991 to the Civil Rights unit of the  Chicago Police Department and his  particular charge was to take action  against hate crimes I think you know  many police departments many many police  officers even might regard this  assignment as a backwater maybe a  demotion something like that but Scalise  knew better and he immediately seized  upon the possibilities in his job it's  important to remember that in 1991 the  concept of hate crimes was rather new  that this was something that people had  not thought about much before in big  city police departments the idea that if  you commit a crime that is motivated by  racial or religious or ethnic bias that  that is something special something a  little different and that it requires  different kinds of enforcement and maybe  ultimately different kinds of punishment  so Scalise  really  took his assignment and grabbed it and  ran with it and he began by doing the  things that make this kind of policing  work preventive policing Scalise began  to meet with all of the minority groups  in Chicago of course he met with people  in the african-american community in the  Latino communities  Chicago of course a very diverse City  more than one Latino community they come  from many different places and he didn't  stop there he also met with many of the  religious groups even some religious  groups that were newer to Chicago and in  each instance he met with these groups  he went out of his way he talked to them  he came to their meetings in each case  his message was the same we're here to  help you we want to know if you've  experienced an incident of bias or  biased crime a hate crime you come to us  we'll help you be our partners will be  your partners and he worked at this very  steadily over the years and had a lot of  success building relationships building  partnerships all as a way of setting up  preventive measures now I want to take  you kind of fast forward to the July 4th  weekend of 1999 this particular weekend  some of you in Chicago may remember this  Scalise got a call in the early evening  he was told come right up immediately to  West Rogers Park or been a shooting and  when he got there he found that there  were six shooting victims six people  who've been shot from a moving car and  he knew right away why he'd been called  as soon as he got there because he saw  the victims there all of them were in  distinctive Orthodox Jewish dress this  is in a Jewish community it was Friday  night the victims all seem to be headed  towards one of the neighborhood  synagogues  it looked like a bias crime it looked  like a hate crime right away and as soon  as he got there he talked to one of his  officers and they told him they had a  problem the problem was the victims  wouldn't talk to the police  nobody in the neighborhood who might  have seen something would talk to the  police  and Scalise knew why because of his long  work with the Jewish community he  understood this he knew it was Friday  night the beginning of the Jewish  Sabbath he knew that observant Jews on  Friday night all the way through the  Sabbath do not do anything that might be  construed as work and because of that  they were not going to talk to municipal  officials even police officers that  might be seen as work now as soon as  this realization set in almost the same  moment another call came in over the  police radio system there had been  another shooting in the nearby suburb of  Skokie a black man outside had been shot  this man died and then just a short time  after that another report from the  suburb of Northbrook a group of Asian  people had been shot at and this told  Scalise he had his worst nightmare right  in front of him  a serial hate crime something you hope  never to see but there it was and he  knew he needed information and he needed  it right now he also knew that until the  Jewish Sabbath ended there would be no  work and therefore no information now he  didn't know what to do about this but he  knew who to call he called a man named  Moshe Wolfe Moshe Wolff is a rabbi lived  in that community was also an Orthodox  Jew he was a chaplain still is as far as  I know a chaplain for the Chicago Police  Department Scalise went to Wolfe he  filled him in  is it can you help me can you help me  can you do something and wolf knew  exactly what to do he went right to the  scene right to where he could find the  victims right to where he could find the  witnesses and he talked to them wolf is  a rabbi he knew exactly what to say  he took the people aside and he  explained to them a Jewish concept  called Baku of nefesh but Kulik nefesh  translates roughly as a matter of life  and death and rabbi wolf explained to  these folks yes we keep the Sabbath we  observe it strictly but a matter of life  and death takes precedence and this is  one of those times and with rabbi Wolf's  encouragement the victims came forward  they went to the 24th district they gave  information about the shooter the car it  was the first fragmentary but the first  description of the shooter and the  vehicle and for the first time the  police were able to match that  information to information that came  from the other shootings they realized  they had the same person and it turned  into a crucial break in the case that  allowed the police to track this shooter  all the way down to downstate Illinois  where they later cornered him it turned  out he was a follower of Matthew Hale of  the World Church of the Creator the  white supremacist Church his name was  Benjamin Smith and it was because  Scalise had a partner it was because he  knew who to go to  that this case took the fortunate turn  that it did that is preventive policing  that is the way police departments can  go in the future to get ahead of crime  to be effective against crime and at the  same time to work with the public to  have them  as partners this idea that police need  more room and citizens need less  constitutional protection in order for  us to be safe Scalise is the proof that  that's not so and in this is not a  theory this is in practice it's on the  ground it's working its effective and  it's measurable preventive policing can  be the future so let me take a few  minutes and tell you just what  preventive policing is there are five  core principles to preventive policing  and if we understand that I think we'll  get a good grip on why it is such an  innovation why it's such an opportunity  first first core principle is building  bridges  this is Scalise building bridges  building partnerships and relationships  with the community so that when the time  comes they're there to be taken  advantage of and used this facilitates  communication and there is simply  nothing more important than  communication because the communication  it takes place between the community  served and the police who serve them  bring lots of important crime-fighting  pieces of information to the police and  they give the police the opportunity to  learn what the community's priorities  are number two problem solving when I  say problem solving what I mean is a  strategy of policing that doesn't focus  on individual incidents it's very easy  in today's world to to talk about how  overwhelmed police are with constant 911  with constant needs to respond and they  are overwhelmed they have more to do  than they can handle  problem solving policing takes a  different approach instead of seeing  each crime as an individual discrete  incident  problem-solving policing looks at the  whole picture pulls back and says well  why do we get so many calls from this  particular corner why so many 911 calls  from this apartment building let's  analyze the data let's look at the crime  patterns over the last 24 months who  were the people involved who were the  victims who were the perpetrators when  we caught them what seemed to be the  patterns here by focusing on patterns  police can devise solutions that will  actually cut down on the number of 911  calls over the long run it will actually  reduce crime and sometimes even  eliminate it from the hot spots in every  city in town  third accountability accountability both  external and internal I cannot emphasize  this strongly enough this is really the  linchpin of so many efforts to reform  and improve policing in so many cities  around the country  when I say external accountability what  I mean is accountability to citizens  when citizens file a complaint is there  an institution a mechanism a device in  place to handle complaints in an  independent way that citizens will trust  when I say internal accountability what  I'm talking about  are new kinds of mechanisms that have  come in to policing over the last 10  years the one that is the most important  in my mind is something we call an early  warning system an early warning system  is a way to use computer technology to  bring discrete pieces of information  about police behavior all together have  them all in one place in one information  system so that way the chief the captain  the lieutenant all the way down to the  sergeant can know what police are doing  individually and contract that behavior  now you'd expect things like citizens  complaints to be measured this way and  tracked this way you might expect thing  such as automobile accidents uses of  force shootings whatever it turns out to  be but there are lots of other things  that go into these systems to Mister  court dates sick days outside employment  all of this creates a total picture of  where any individual officer is at and  when the lieutenant the sergeant the  captain sees signs of trouble they can  nip that trouble in the bud they can get  the officer retrained they can get the  officer counsel they can send the  officer to substance abuse treatment  whatever it takes they can get things  back on track and what if what it takes  is nudging somebody into another line of  work they might be able to do that too  leadership changes for many many years  police leadership has been same-old  same-old in a military-style  organization you know police have ranks  there are lines of authority there are  reporting systems don't go over the  captain's head to the next guy and  increasingly there are new leaders in  police leaders who adapt and who have to  have brought to policing a different  style and a key ingredient here is  openness an openness to new ideas and  openness to outsiders and most most  different of all a willingness to share  power in police organizations for many  many years  the idea was accumulate power for  yourself no matter what your position  was you don't want to give it away and  an increasing number of Chiefs are  seeing that the thing to do is to give  their subordinates power along with  accountability and responsibility this  is a sea change in law enforcement  number five changes in police culture  this may be the toughest nut to crack of  all you know police culture is  notoriously insular police organizations  are famous for excluding the ideas and  views of outsiders there's a real  feeling that  you haven't worn the uniform or carried  the badge you don't know what the job is  like and you really have no right to  criticize I understand where that comes  from it is a singular kind of job but  ultimately it's a job in a democratic  society in which the public has to be  served this realization that is dawning  on an increasing number of police  departments is what is driving changes  in police culture and what I see  could be neatly encapsulated you know in  a nice little analogy I came across I  saw an article by by somebody the title  of it it was about changing police  culture in it then the article said  something like it's changing police  culture will be just as easy as bending  granite easy bending granite and I get  it  you know there's there's something to  that but what I also see is that now we  could not we could regard police culture  not as granite but maybe is wood wood is  hard wood is firm but wood can be bent  if you take the time you apply the  pressure correctly a strong straight  piece of wood can become the beautiful  curving wood of the guitar or a violin  and that's the kind of thing it will  take to change police culture strong  consistent pressure over a long time a  demand that things be done in a new and  better way and now that there are models  out there this is no longer a pipe dream  so those are the five core ideas  building bridges solving problems  accountability leadership changes and  changes in police culture I think what  I'd like to do is to just illustrate one  of these for you using something I saw  while I was researching and writing the  book because when you go out and you  look at these police departments  and you see what they're actually doing  it really is remarkable it really shows  you that change is possible in an area  where we often thought it was hopeless  so let me take a few minutes and tell  you about Stamford Connecticut Stamford  Connecticut this is a medium-sized city  outside of New York and in the middle to  late 90s they got a new chief Chiefs  name is Dean Esserman  chief Esserman  was known as an original thinker in  policing a person who was open to ideas  so one of the reasons I like this story  is it brings a number of different  principles together one of the things  that chief Esserman did when he started  in Stanford was he began a school police  officer program lots of communities have  these programs they are formally known  as SRO program school resource officer  programs there is funding available  there's training available there are  standards things like that set out by  federal agencies and organizations  Esserman liked this idea he liked the  fact that police would be able to  connect with the children in the  community that they'd be able to connect  with the community through the schools  and he decided he wanted to have a  program of school resource officers in  his town so he picked out his officers  and the people he wanted for this  program he did not go for what you might  think as you know the conventional  choices he didn't look for people who  had maybe special skills with kids he  went for his toughest biggest most  experienced cops cops and two of the  many came up with were Scott Baldwin and  John Jeter they're fascinating guys  Baldwin had been on the dive team of the  department he'd been in a SWAT unit I  mean this was an action-oriented police  officer  no mistaking it John Jeter had been in  Newark in the narcotics squad for 15  years very successful and so nobody  could say that these were cops who  couldn't do the whole job that's why a  sermon wanted them that's why he  selected them well these officers along  with eight others went into the Stanford  schools and the program was an immediate  success the community loved it schools  loved it and the kids loved it but maybe  most surprising the cops loved it the  police officers just loved it and you  talked to John Jeter about this and he  he says you know this this was the  greatest thing that ever happened to me  I was in narcotics for 15 years I was  you know out there on the street I was  making a bus here I got a chance to  prevent future crime I got a chance to  get ahead and that's just not something  you can do when you're around the  narcotics squad John Jeter was posted to  a middle school and he became one of the  most popular police officers in Stanford  well he went through his first year well  into the second year and chief Esserman  held a meeting with the school resource  officers toward the end of that second  year he held periodic meetings with all  his officers so he'd get to see them  talk to them take take stock of their  experience get their ideas and he was  known as being an original thinker in a  kind of open-minded chief so when chief  Esserman  said to the school resource officers at  this meeting what are you going to do  this summer  John Jeter decided to take a chance  because he had a wild idea he decided to  let this idea out and see how the chief  reacted to it he said chief how about we  start a summer camp how about we have a  summer camp for the kids in my school  and he explained you know he said you  know these are middle school kids the  ones who go to summer school will go to  summer school till noon every day by the  time it's noon  it's too late for them to go to the  regular camps they all start in the  morning they're too young to get summer  jobs the too old for the daycare stuff  so what happens to them loose on the  street loose at home parents still at  work it's a recipe for disaster  I want to figure out something we can do  for my kids my kids he says hundreds of  my kids and I want to have a camp we  could have it here at school we can give  the kids lunch you know we'll do  something with them we'll figure it out  now I think that in most police  departments the idea of let's run a  summer camp for middle schoolers might  have seemed crazy I think a lot of  Chiefs would have reacted badly to this  what are you nuts we're cops we're not  camp counselors what do you think this  is but estimate is a different kind of  chief and his reaction instantly was  let's do it this is a great idea and he  said about building on the relationships  that he had already cultivated in his  two or so years as chief he went to the  mayor and got a commitment of city funds  so that they could hire high school kids  and college kids as counselors pay him a  good wage give him something to put on a  resume give him training he went to a  local foundation a Domus foundation this  foundation had been sponsoring youth  programs in Stanford for a long time  they were the right organization because  they could supply the administrative  structure for the camp as you know  police don't really know how to run a  camp but these people do they went to  the to the City School District they got  the district to commit the use of one of  their buildings they got the bus service  committed and they got private sector  money to fill out the picture and last  but not least a sermon ever the taker of  chances he takes his drug forfeiture  money which is supposed to go to buy  fancy equipment for his department says  just going into camp and I don't care  what you say about it  and so the camp was often running in his  first year it serviced a hundred kids  the next year more the year after that  even more by the third year was taking  every kid in town that wanted to be in  this camp I visited this camp it was  really something to see you know the  kids are well occupied they're doing all  the things that you do in a camp some  are taking martial arts and some are  doing computers and some are doing arts  and crafts and some are outside playing  ball but they all are doing something  and they all are doing it with people  who can be good strong adult role models  and it was really something to see all  these high school kids the college kids  they all had good jobs for the summer  there was competition to get these jobs  after the first year it was a real  success so you ask yourself how is this  crime fighting how does this fight crime  it's a legitimate question after the  first two years the United States  Department of Justice Unit came in and  did an evaluation of the camp they found  that kids were more able to resist gang  recruitment drug use had better  relationships with the police and that  the police had better relationships not  only with the kids but with the parents  and the community that the relationships  that had flowered during the school year  actually got even better but again you  ask yourself show me that it works for  cutting crime is this crime fighting or  is it social work is it camp counseling  well let me show you we have a very  interesting opportunity to learn  something from this experience because  after the first several years that the  camp was in operation in 2002 the bottom  fell out of the economy  and Stamford Connecticut as it did in  many other cities in the country private  sector money for the camp completely  dried up the city administration had no  extra money all the school district  could do would be to commit what was to  commit the school building and even the  foundation the Domus foundation was  unable to do anything but a kind of  scaled-down effort and so when the camp  was looking at the summer of 2003 things  looked pretty bleak and what they  decided that they wanted to do was to  preserve the structure of the camp but  it would have to be in a much  scaled-down sighs so the camp of 2003  was less than 20 percent the size of the  camp of 2002 they were down to about 35  kids and these were the 35 kids were  most at risk that we're going to the  at-risk summer program and what this  does for us standing back now is it  gives us an opportunity to ask a pretty  interesting question no it's not a  controlled experiment but if we can get  the numbers we can actually see what  difference the camp might have made so  let me show you some of these numbers  in the summer of 2001 in Stanford there  were 69 arrests of juveniles kids in  this age bracket for two hundred and  thirty six crimes in the summer of 2002  the trajectory which had been started  actually before what you see here was  clearly downward for only fifty seven  arrests for a hundred and seventy five  total crimes now we look at 2003 the  year in which the camp was vastly scaled  back a hundred and five juvenile arrests  for four hundred and fourteen separate  crimes now I'm not going to stand up  here and make the logical mistake of  saying just because something happened  after it happened because of but I have  looked and looked I have to tell you  four other explanations of why crime  suddenly spiked up in this age group in  this particular summer and I can't find  one it seems at least a question worth  asking at least something worth  exploring and I'm hoping that as we go  forward to 2005 the camp is going to be  up and running again at full strength  because this was a perfect example of  problem solving by a police officer  Jeter looks out he sees a problem he  sees hundreds of kids with nothing to do  for five hours a day before at least  some of their parents come home between  12:00 and 5 he knows that's the most  crime prone hours for this age group  this is when kids get into trouble he  looks at the whole picture and he says  what would change this and what would  allow me to solidify my relationships  with my kids and he comes up with this  idea the camp  this is problem-solving and preventive  policing at its absolute finest and it's  rightly regarded as a national example  of the kind of things that police can do  when they're allowed creativity when  they're told big picture  take a look ahead prevent don't just  react so much of our policing for so  long has been about reacting to crime  reacting after its occurred now that  will never go away they're always going  to be emergencies it's always going to  be 911 police respond to that's never  going to go away but what we can look  forward to with preventive policing is a  future where that's not all the police  do and they put an increasing amount of  effort to reducing crime over the long  haul in ways that will frankly make for  fewer emergencies as we go on now let's  talk about today's world and the  question that's on everybody's mind what  about terrorism does preventive policing  have a role in preventing terrorism how  is it stack up there and the short  answer is preventive policing prevention  is what the whole effort is about with  terrorism that's what the whole game is  you know I hope that they will catch  Osama bin Laden at some point I hope  that will happen I hope he faces justice  for what he did I hope all the people  that helped him do that will face  justice but frankly the damage is done  whether they catch him or not  what would be really good is to be able  to head off further terrorist attacks  prevent them whether we catch people  afterwards when the damage has been done  much better to catch him beforehand so  prevention is the right focus and it  turns out that preventive policing is  exactly the strategy  that can do this for us and that  reactive types of policing don't help at  all and in fact may even hurt us now  when we say preventive policing and  terrorism the most important thing the  most a number one issue for preventive  policing for prevention of terrorism is  information I don't mean anything fancy  here you can call it intelligence if  you're the CIA and the National Security  Agency  I mean information in the sense that  police officers use that word on the  ground you want to know what's going on  in your community you have to talk to  the people in your community you have to  have relationships with them you have to  have a two-way information conduit  between the people you serve and the  people doing the serving information is  the key the absolute essential to  heading off terrorism think about it I  mean if we have sleeper cells on our  soil right now if there are cells in  this country made up of middle-eastern  adherents of Osama bin Laden if they're  there and a government says they are  what we need is information there are  only a couple of ways to figure out what  these people are up to one is  infiltrating them but since the FBI has  fired all its arabic-speaking agents and  has trouble with its translators and  just can't recruit people from these  communities that's out so we have one  choice there is only one possibility for  getting this information just one we  have to get it from the communities  themselves they are the people who know  the language they are the people who  know the culture they are the people who  understand the nuances of what people  say and how they express themselves  who's moving in who's moving out who's  keeping a low profile  some odd way who's got suspicious  opinions whatever it happens to be the  people in the community are the only  ones that we can turn to now we need the  middle eastern communities like we have  never needed anyone in fact for  prevention of terrorism how are we going  to accomplish this preventive policing  build those bridges solve problems with  the community be accountable to them you  make a mistake say so when you have  statistics that show the performance of  your department put them out there let  the public react to them take complaints  seriously and hold your officers  accountable for them those are the  things that will allow people to begin  to trust you  and to treat you as a partner if you're  the police department and that's what  will bring you the information and  frankly it's the only thing that can  bring you the information because if the  people in the community don't trust the  police they'll fear the police and that  will break down that conduit of  information let me give you an example  of how this works we're all familiar I  hope with the cell that was broken in  Lackawanna New York this is claimed by  the Department of Justice to be the  single biggest victory in the war on  terror on American soil Lackawanna is a  small town outside of Buffalo New York  it is it has a very large population of  people who came from Yemen and when the  cell hit the newspapers when the fact of  these arrests hit the newspapers there  was some information that was not  noticed sometimes even left out as some  of these reports how did the government  break the lock Awana cell was it FBI  wiretap was it the CIA was it the  National Security  see intercepting communications and  emails and so forth no none of that the  way the government broke this case was  the community itself members of the  Yemeni community came to law enforcement  before law enforcement was even aware  that there might be a problem in little  Lackawanna New York the community  the Yemeni community came to them  including that came to the community  policing officer for this town the  person they knew and they said look six  or seven young guys they seemed okay  they play soccer but we think you should  know all of them went to Camp Osama last  summer we don't know what this is about  we don't you know none of us were there  but you should know because maybe you  want to look into this that was the key  break in the case without that this  doesn't even occur there's no break in  the case and for all we know that cell  is still operating so prevention turns  out to be the absolute key aspect in  fighting terrorism and it's often  overlooked now that would be a great  place to be able to stop and say it's  the future you know this is where we're  going yes they'll always be emergencies  and things like that we'll always have  to respond in some way but preventive  policing is the way to get ahead of  crime this is clearly how we can make  our communities better how we can tie  the communities and police together how  we can have policing that is effective  that does not force us to make that  compromise of giving up what's best  about being an American and I wish that  that was really all I had to  about all of this but it's not you know  when you when you do a project like this  when you write a book you have to be  prepared to be surprised you have to be  prepared to learn things maybe you  didn't expect and that happened to me I  was moving around the country I was  visiting police departments I was going  here and going there seeing some very  impressive police work but about a year  and a half ago about two years ago I  began to hear something I didn't expect  and it came from Chiefs it came from  captains it came from patrol officers on  the street they began to tell me  something that I did not expect they  said we've got a problem because there  is a counter trend there is something  else happening here we've got the  federal government asking that we do  some things that don't seem all that  smart to us and what they're trying to  do is instead of taking up these new  positive initiatives they're beginning  to rely on the old style traditional  dragnet types of enforcement and the  federal government does not seem to be  aware or even care that we the local  police the people on the ground  understand how to police in an immigrant  community understand how to grab up  intelligence and have relationships  instead they're going back to the bad  old ways of the bad old days and they  want to take us with them and I was  really kind of surprised to hear this  but I got example after example from  them let's give you one or two this  counter-trend can be seen most clearly  in federal efforts to force local police  into immigration work  there's a big problem in this country as  you know with immigration immigration  system is broken it's broken  everybody agrees what the federal  government wants to do is to take local  police and State Police and use them as  a quote force multiplier to use them as  adjuncts to the inss or whatever the I  NS is called now in their new homeland  security guys they want local police to  enforce immigration law on behalf of the  federal government and local police know  how dangerous a situation and how crazy  a suggestion that really is they the  federal government want to give any more  training I want to give them any more  funding I'm particularly sensitive to  the idea of training I'm a law professor  I don't teach immigration laws not my  area and I would no more give somebody  immigration advice then you know advice  on how to become King of England or  something it's complicated it's like tax  law changes every two years it's  incredibly detailed and they want to  send local police officers out into the  field and have them enforce immigration  law with no training no standards  nothing local police know that this is a  recipe for disaster they have been the  ones who have built the bridges with  their immigrant communities they know  that if they want the streets to be safe  in Chicago or in Austin Texas or in Los  Angeles or in many other places around  the country they have to work with the  immigrant communities whether people are  legal or illegal whether they're in the  country legitimately or not the police  have made efforts in many cities around  the country to have the population  understand have the people understand  that they're not interested in their  immigration status they are interested  in making  them safe local police want to secure  safety for everybody in the city because  they're all human beings matter them  what their legal status is and they know  that if they go out into the community  and they start playing AI NS and they  start arresting people for being out of  status for being illegal the community  will begin to fear them they will stop  reporting crimes in the community they  will stop reporting when even they are  victimized when they are robbery victims  when they are rape victims when they are  victims of domestic violence people  won't come to them they won't call the  police they won't even call the fire  department if they think they're going  to get reported so there's been a real  strong effort over the last five years  by local police in many places to show  the immigrant communities we're on your  side we don't care about that stuff we  want to make our city safe because we  are all against the Predators who are  out there and what the federal effort to  push local police into immigration work  will do is destroy these efforts set  them back if good policing preventive  policing is about building bridges this  will be about burning bridges and once  that's done you can't get it back other  examples the federal government's effort  to question 5,000 young men from the  Arab world where in the United States  began in fall of 2001 another 3,000 in  the fall of 2002 why do they want to  question them well they're not suspects  it's all voluntary they might just might  know something  and while the Attorney General can give  orders to the FBI to go out there and do  this  they asked because they could not force  they asked local police to get involved  too because only the local police would  know their communities well enough to be  able to go into those communities and  find the people the Department of  Justice was seeking  and the local police departments did not  sign up some did but there was a  surprising amount of opposition some of  it out loud some of it very quiet lots  of big city and small town police  chief's saying you know what we don't  want any part of this you got to do this  you go out and do it yourself  we don't want it because this will just  put us on the wrong side and all that  information that we've been getting will  begin to disappear and we just don't  want that and we're going to be the ones  we the local police will be the ones who  in five years or ten years end up on the  wrong side of the lawsuit that gets  brought because of this while you folks  in the Department of Justice go on your  merry way will be trapped in these  failures and you'll probably sue us too  they don't want any part of that so  the net effect here is a disaster on the  Terrorism front  the net effect has been to set back  anti-terrorism work in a big and  noticeable way this is a big loss for  all of us not just for the communities  affected but for anybody really cares  about being safe from terrorism and it's  because the federal government has not  been keen enough to instead of talk to  instead of telling the local police  here's what you're going to do for us  imagine if they'd gone to local police  how do you get into information out of  your inner immigrant communities how do  you build relationships with them we  don't know anybody in the FBI we don't  know anybody in the Muslim community in  Dearborn Michigan how do we do that  wouldn't that have been a good thing but  instead the United States Department of  Justice has charged ahead in precisely  the wrong direction so how do we get  preventive policing you know I want to  leave us with the feeling that there's  something that we can do because I think  there is the first thing you have to do  the first thing that has to happen is  for the public at large to understand  what preventive policing is to  understand what it is so that you can  recognize it and see it in your own  communities when it happens I found this  happening in 30 states cities towns  east-west Midwest south it happens  everywhere but it gets very little  attention what most people know in the  United States is the Giuliani New York  story right you want to cut crime you  want to make the streets safe you have  to do like Rudolph Giuliani did in New  York you got to go out there and be  tough and have zero tolerance and make  lots and lots of arrests for lots of  small offenses because that's will keep  the big offenses  from happening now the media knows that  story and the media is centered in New  York so that's why we all know that  story there are legitimate reasons to  wonder whether that's what was actually  responsible for bringing crime down in  New York that's a different talk for a  different day but what I can tell you  for sure is that there's a new way of  doing things that we don't have to put  up with massive numbers of arrests and  tough tough tough enforcement in order  to have safer streets and I saw the  proof of it over and over and over in  many places in this country so the first  step is to understand it the second is  to support it when you see things like  this happening in your community when  you see police officers come into  community meetings and commit it over  the long term and willing to sit down  and listen to the community when you see  that you need to support it you need to  talk to your City Council people and say  you know the following happened last  week that's good I want to see more of  that  you know the officer got out of his car  and he talked with those five kids on  the corner instead of giving them an  order out of the PA he had a  conversation with them I want to see  more of that that took that took guts to  do that and I want to see more of it  when you do that you have a chance of  getting it and last but not least there  is simply no substitute for demanding it  if we don't demand better police work we  won't get it now  oftentimes that will take the form of  demanding that certain practices stop  that excessive use of force be stopped  that there be new training so that the  worst that policing sometimes brings to  us will go away will be eliminated but  it takes more than that you have to be  able to say not only do I not want this  I want this  and the examples of preventive policing  that I've been talking about are the  kinds of things that everybody in this  country can support and must demand  because that's the only way we're going  to get it now the good thing is I found  lots of police officers and lots of  leaders in police who were willing to do  it but if they don't get support frankly  from us they won't be able to do it so  it takes not just them it takes us to  it's out there it's not a theory it can  be done it's being done and it's  succeeding I'm ready for your questions  thank you  thank you thank you very much we have a  mic that I think will pick up the  questions in the crowd if you have  questions please raise your hand  sure  could you comment professor Harris on  the on the onion unions on police union  work and how that may affect what you  would like to have done question was  about police unions how would it affect  efforts at preventive policing it's very  good question  I said when I was discussing what  preventive policing was about that one  of the most important of the five  principles was accountability and I have  to say that over the long haul police  organizations don't have a great record  for supporting accountability to their  communities that they have often and  harshly fought against efforts like  citizen review boards in many cities  around the country those boards in fact  have a very mixed record sometimes  because of police union opposition and  sometimes because the boards are set up  without what they need to function  correctly such as subpoena powers such  as a budget such as a staff such as  independence most importantly and police  unions in some areas of the country have  been at the forefront of stymieing many  accountability efforts when those  efforts include civilian input now I I'm  not impugning police unions I don't mean  to do that at all there are many labor  issues for which police unions are  critical for police officers the  membership of these unions had  legitimate issues that they need to have  protected in negotiations with the city  in which they operate at the same time  unions have sometimes become a block to  reform to changes that everybody agrees  are needed and I don't think anybody has  an interest in that this goes back to  something else I said  there's a feeling that if you haven't  been on the job you haven't worn the  uniform you don't have standing to  criticize therefore civilian review  really has no place I simply don't agree  with that in fact civilian review is  necessary for the credibility of  policing for the public to believe in  policing to have faith in its police  department to have face that faith that  its officers are in the same team as the  public is that kind of credibility is  crucial and without accountability you  just don't have it  next question this man there's been like  a long history of bad blood so to speak  in negativity between the police and the  residents what are some of the things  they police can do to signal that they  are in fact trying to go about a  different way because there's such a  deep mistrust of the officers and let me  repeat the question that's a great  question in many communities a strong  historical distrust exists between  police and communities and it runs both  ways it really does in a situation like  that what can police departments do to  recover some of that trust - to start  over again if you like to build upon it  in order to make some of these  preventive measures possible it's a very  good question and it's a very difficult  thing to do but it's not impossible I  would point to the example of Boston a  lot has been written about the very  successful efforts in Boston to curb  juvenile homicides with guns but this  was part of a larger picture if you go  back to Boston in the late 80s and early  90s there was really almost no city in  the country in which there was deeper  distrust between police and the  african-american community than in  Boston I bet a lot of you remember the  story of Charles Stewart and his wife  I'll just remind you this was a white  man who's at the hospital with his  pregnant wife also white  he shot his wife in their car shot  himself reported to the police a black  guy jumped out was trying to rob us and  shot us both the police took you know  took his report as gospel and began to  go through Roxbury and all the the other  black neighborhoods in Boston just  turned every young black man upside down  you know just really made a scene of it  and in the end of course what had  happened was the Charles Stewart had  done this and he threw himself over over  a bridge into the Charles River so he  never had to face the consequences but  it was this and a number of other very  very poor incidents that really had  police and community relations in Boston  at a very low point in the early 90s and  then at the same time they began to face  this terrible problem with their kids  killing each other shooting each other  with handguns and there was a hundred  and fifty such killings in one of the  worst years alone and they had no idea  what to do the old methods that you know  going to the neighborhoods with you know  like a bulldozer and frisk everybody and  sweep the corners none that stuff is  working so maybe what it takes is  getting to a point where you're just  searching for answers because what they  came up with was a unique combination of  resources and people they brought  together not just the highest levels of  law enforcement in the city of Police  Department the United States attorney  the district attorney the probation  department which never usually has  anything to do with the police  department believe it or not along with  the faith community the 10-point  coalition a coalition of black ministers  people in a school system lots and lots  of people and they got together and they  said what do we really have here what do  we really have here it was a  problem-solving workshop essentially  that covered many months what they did  was they figured out what the problem  was  took a one step at a time it turned out  you look at the statistics you looked at  the shootings you looked at the  incidents what you had was a hardcore  small number of gang members it wasn't  even all the gang members it was just  10% they were causing all this havoc so  they brought these people around the  table all the people from the community  from law enforcement they put together a  rounded effort they didn't just go out  and round people up what they did was  they created alternatives anybody wants  to get out of the gang life we can find  you a GED program and get you back into  high school and find you a job we can  get you trained we can do something for  you if you want to get out this is your  opportunity with the hardcore 10% those  people were called in a few at a time  they sat in a room with this  high-powered law enforcement team and  they were told in no uncertain terms no  sheriff in town things are going to  change there's going to be no more guns  we catch you with a gun got you're  shooting a gun catch you're shooting at  somebody with a gun you are going to do  federal time will not be down with  people from the neighborhood and the  county jail you will not go into Western  mass into one of those jails you'll be  out some godforsaken place in Montana or  Nevada where you don't know nobody  everybody's bigger than you and they  hate your guts and this got people's  attention within a month these homicides  went directly downward for two and a  half years there were no homicides by  gun between juveniles now they're more  than zero now things have not stayed  that peaceful they're back to a level  that is not tolerable  but is a little more normal than where  they were the point is this folks in the  community and the police thought they  had nothing in common they thought  they'd had it with each other and by  working together to solve the problem by  asking themselves what's the real  problem here they  they had common ground and they were  able to break through all of that Boston  is now one of the places that other  police departments come to to ask how do  we handle a problem like this so it can  be done it takes work it takes  commitment takes patience it takes time  you know you don't get into situations  of deep distress like that overnight you  don't get out of them overnight either  as people who work in that community  where that historic ingrained mistrust  has been there and it takes us a very  long time to even gain the trust to come  into the community to do the work that  we need to do or that we want to do from  the whole house how do we bring these  two people together because we actually  straddle working with the police even  getting the police to acknowledge us  that we're coming into these  developments this community to do good  stuff and the residents are trusting us  to do this stuff without kind of them  thinking we're telling on you we're  telling you what they're doing so where  does it start  because we we seem to get to a point and  then we just stand there you know we  just get a police officer to come in to  show good faith see this is officer  so-and-so and she's working with us from  CPD but that's as far as it goes  that police officer can't then take it  back to a bigger arena even in a station  you know which where she works from to  help us continue to do the work that  needs to be done because primary is what  you're talking about to get the police  to do preventive stuff and to gain the  support and trust of that community it's  almost impossible yes a very good  question I don't want to do too much to  reframe it because I think you really  hit so many things on the head there  first of all let me say in reaction I  honor your service and the work you do  it's extremely difficult you're taking  risks you're going out on a limb  personally and I recognize that and I  honor what you're doing for your  communities and without people like you  know  thing would happen so the first step has  actually already been taken and it's  been taken by you and I think everybody  owes you thanks how do we get past that  it's tough but the only way that that  can happen is if there's real commitment  on the part of the police department  you've shown coming from Hull House  coming from the community  you've shown you're willing to step up  offer your hand get involved it can't  end with the department simply sending  an officer for a meeting that is not  enough if the police department and the  other citizens in this city want to see  real change they want to see crime rates  go down and stay down and they want to  see that happen not just in their own  neighborhoods but across the city in  neighborhoods where they don't live  there has to be a citywide commitment  the police department must must follow  through on those things if they don't it  will fail that's the surest thing I can  tell you so I don't have a real  optimistic message I just say takes work  and the work has to come from both sides  you're doing it let's see some more  question over here  my question has to do with your attitude  that's the wrong word with your view of  the Justice Department's efforts to  involve local police departments in  their work and I was in I was just  wondering if there is any change or if  there's any movement in the in the  Justice Department or the federal  government  to see your point of view and to okay to  adjust or modify those rather stringent  directives that you that you that you  criticized so much sure the question is  is there any change of foot any prospect  of things getting different in terms of  how the Justice Department is seeking to  use and work with local police I do see  a little bit of change but I see it on  the ground  I see it in the FBI field offices I  talked about Dearborn before so it seems  only fair to start there the FBI office  in the Eastern District of Michigan  where Dearborn is near Detroit has been  very active with trying to work with the  Muslim community to do things with them  and in fact I know that the the special  agent in charge of the FBI field office  until recently and the fellow who was  the United States Attorney these are  people who are in the supervisory roles  on the ground they nominated for  instance one of the Arab Americans most  communities most prominent activists for  a special Justice Department award to  recognize the great work he had done  mobilizing his community to work with  the FBI so they wanted to recognize him  to further pull these efforts together  and I'm sorry to report that this did  not work out the way that either of  them hoped the FBI director director  Muller rejected this nomination after it  had been announced for undisclosed  reasons reasons that still aren't  disclosed but of course leave the kind  of patina that this man may have been  somehow vaguely connected with terrorism  is a terrible thing and as you can guess  it really worsened relations in a way  that you almost can't imagine so I see  the people on the ground really trying  to do things better trying to go into  those communities and and create the  relationships and so forth they're not  getting a great deal of support in  Washington from what I can tell now the  obvious question that kind of pops into  your mind is what about now John  Ashcroft is gone Albert Alberto Gonzalez  is the Attorney General maybe things  will improve I see him as a sort of  kinder gentler Attorney General he says  I read that he's reaching out to the  Justice Department senior staff the  career people that scene is a good thing  but you know in his first major policy  address on these questions in the end of  February  he basically announced that he was  supporting every initiative that the  prior Attorney General had put forward  so the best I can tell you on the top  level is wait and see I don't see any  evidence that things have changed yet  your to your to the question from the  lady from hull-house I gathered that  municipal and police leadership is  essential to preventive policing and  then when I hear your story about which  I read about in the book and Stamford  Connecticut  I'm concerned because we are in an era  where municipalities and police  departments frankly are struggling to  come up with enough money just to pay  their police officers and there's a  great aspect of print preventative  policing that frankly requires money and  in in this era how what's your honest  forecast in terms of how do we sell this  to municipal leaders into police leaders  who are struggling to come up with  enough pennies to pay their officers  much less put those officers in  alternative programs or paid for  alternative programs and whatnot it's an  excellent question where is the money  going to come from you know in American  society that's always something we have  to think about and it's a very live  question right now because the federal  budget which is in front of Congress  which came from President Bush some  weeks back that budget supplies a good  deal of seed money for programs like the  ones I've been talking about and for  reasons that really escaped me these  programs and others are on the chopping  block they have been either zero funded  or ninety percent cut things like that  so it's clear that the rescue is not  going to come from the federal  government in fact the opposite is going  to happen how do you sell it you have to  sell it and I don't think it's any it's  any stretch to sell it as public safety  it is public safety  these aren't extras these aren't add-ons  these are ways to create and maintain  public safety over the long run great  example I heard last week I was  listening to the news there is a group  in California that is proposing  mandatory I'm not sorry not mandatory  but universal preschool preschool for  every four-year-old kid it would be  universally available it's now of course  available to those  kanpei but this would make it available  to everybody the strongest supporters of  this of this initiative and it's going  to be on the